{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790293028,"nonce":"n3fAcAPedpYIHXd1jV2ccU39118=","title":"Lua: a loaded chunk still costs about 1 ms, lowered and compiled with Cranelift at load","message":"After the joined-load change a load no longer re-types or re-adopts the library: the chunk is lowered against a selective import of the library functions it names, links the installed library functions and globals where they are, and is compiled at once at the baseline tier (TieredRuntime::join_typed_program). On the NUC, 200 loads of `return \u003ci\u003e + 1` take 0.18 to 0.21 s inside the loop (lua5.4: 0.3 ms), down from 6.0 s; 2000 loads 1.7 s, near linear. The target was 200 loads under 50 ms.\n\nWhat is left per load, from gdb stack samples of 6000 loads: HIR lowering about half (LoweringContext construction and drop, typecheck and declaration collection over the 50-odd kept declarations, the type registry clone the frontend and the import each make), Cranelift codegen of the chunk's three functions (chunk, chunk$fn, init) about a third, run_interp_safe_opts the rest.\n\nGetting under 0.25 ms needs the chunk not compiled at load: run on the interpreter tier first, as the brief for 63840a4 asked. That is blocked on reentrancy: the main program runs inside `interpreter.lock().call(...)` for its whole run (TieredRuntime::call_raw), so a load called from it cannot interpret the chunk with that interpreter; the chunk is entered by native pointer. Options: a second interpreter instance per nested entry, bound with the same symbols and global slots, plus a native-to-interpreter trampoline for the chunk's function values; or the chunk's init and first calls run through the lazy stub path without compiling (a stub that interprets). Joined chunks also never tier up (no OSR probes, not registered with the promotion requester), so hot loaded code stays at the baseline.","files":null}]}